  "answer_text": "There are **three ways** to change the color of a `<span>` on mouse over in HTML. Here they are, from simplest to most flexible:\n\n---\n\n## \u2705 Method 1: Pure CSS (Recommended)\n\nIf you can identify an element by ID (`#`) or class (`.`), you can add the `:hover` selector to make changes only appear when the user has moused over an element.\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <head>\n    <style>\n      span:hover {\n        color: red;\n      }\n    </style>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <span>Hover over me!</span>\n  </body>\n</html>\n```\n\nYou can also target a specific span using a class or ID:\n\n```css\n/* Using a class */\n.my-span:hover {\n  color: blue;\n}\n\n/* Using an ID */\n#my-span:hover {\n  color: green;\n}\n```\n\nThis is the **cleanest and most recommended** approach.\n\n---\n\n## \u2705 Method 2: Inline JavaScript (Quick but not recommended)\n\nThe JavaScript code must be attached inside quotes to an HTML tag using event handlers like `onmouseover` and `onmouseout`, which replace the CSS selector `:hover`.\n\n```html\n<span\n  onmouseover=\"this.style.color='red';\"\n  onmouseout=\"this.style.color='black';\"\n>\n  Hover over me!\n</span>\n```\n\nInside those quotes, translating CSS to JavaScript requires these conversions: use the JavaScript keyword `this` to refer to the HTML element the user is interacting with, add the property `.style.` onto the element, and convert hyphenated CSS attributes (like `background-color`) to camelCase JavaScript ones (like `backgroundColor`).\n\n> \u26a0\ufe0f Adding JavaScript inside HTML tags (\"inline\") is inflexible, time-consuming, and klunky.\n\n---\n\n## \u2705 Method 3: External JavaScript (Most flexible)\n\nThe `onmouseover` event is triggered when the user hovers over an element with the mouse. You can attach it cleanly via JavaScript:\n\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html>\n  <body>\n    <span id=\"mySpan\">Hover over me!</span>\n\n    <script>\n      const span = document.getElementById(\"mySpan\");\n\n      span.addEventListener(\"mouseover\", function () {\n        this.style.color = \"red\";\n      });\n\n      span.addEventListener(\"mouseout\", function () {\n        this.style.color = \"black\"; // Revert color when mouse leaves\n      });\n    </script>\n  </body>\n</html>\n```\n\nThe `mouseover` event is used to change the color of an element. To restore the color after removing the mouse from the element, the `mouseout` event is also used, which fires when the mouse pointer is removed from the element.\n\n---\n\n## \ud83c\udfc6 Summary\n\n| Method | Ease | Maintainability |\n|---|---|---|\n| CSS `:hover` | \u2b50 Easiest | \u2705 Best |\n| Inline JavaScript | \u2b50 Easy | \u274c Poor |\n| External JavaScript | \u2b50\u2b50 Moderate | \u2705 Great |\n\n**CSS `:hover`** is the go-to choice for simply changing a color on mouse over. Use JavaScript only when you need dynamic or conditional behavior.",
